## Context

Ticket 0330 made the Hartigan dip test run for the first time. It does not
reject unimodality anywhere: p=1.0000 on the full corpus (n=29,675) and 0.99 or
above in each period, with a dip statistic of 0.00098 — roughly six times below
the 1/sqrt(n) null scale. The diagnostic explains the disagreement with
delta_bic: the axis score is skewed (0.59) and leptokurtic (excess 1.11), and
the two-component GMM that wins delta_bic=1355 has its components 0.84 sigma
apart, giving one mode in the fitted density. A two-Gaussian mixture needs about
2 sigma of separation to have two humps.

Section 5.3 of `deliverables/multilayer/multilayer-detection.qmd` currently
opens "The embedding layer reveals a bimodal structure in the post-2015
corpus." That is the claim the dip test does not support.

The finding is narrower than "the poles do not exist", and more interesting:
the two vocabularies are real and separable, but the literature is a continuum
along them rather than two camps. That is consistent with the venue
concentration, with the embedding/lexical agreement at r=0.77, and with the
Œconomia manuscript's own line that the poles "are not external to each other.
They use the same categories but interpret them differently."

The Œconomia manuscript was checked and is not exposed: it quotes no `bim_*`
variable, its two-pole material is historical, and A.6 validates the axis by
venue ecology rather than by any distributional claim. The multilayer paper is
not on a live publication track either (STATE.md lists only RDJ-26561 and
Œconomia). So this is a correctness fix to a dormant deliverable, not an
erratum.

## Why this is not a one-line prose edit

The accepted rewrite states a dip p-value, a component separation and a mode
count. None of the three exists as a pipeline variable:

- `compute_vars.py::bimodality_stats` never reads `dip_pvalue`, so no
  `bim_dip_*` variable exists.
- Nothing computes the separation or the mode count of the fitted mixture. They
  came from a throwaway diagnostic during 0330.

Hardcoding them into the prose would break the project rule that numbers in
prose are vars-driven — the rule that already cost this project the 28%→24% and
27%/47% rot. So the pipeline emits them first, and the prose reads them.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/analysis/analyze_bimodality.py` — must emit separation and mode count
- `scripts/analysis/compute_vars.py::bimodality_stats` — must expose dip and
  shape variables
- `deliverables/multilayer/multilayer-detection.qmd` — §5.3, and the §5.4
  closing clause that leans on it
- `tests/test_multilayer_detection_pca.py` — pins §5.3 by heading text and
  asserts positive phrasings

## Actions

1. `_gmm_delta_bic` returns the fitted 2-component model's shape as well:
   standardised separation between component means, and the number of modes in
   the fitted density counted on a fine grid. New columns
   `gmm_separation_sigma` and `gmm_n_modes` on the embedding and per-period rows
   of `tab_bimodality.csv`.
2. `bimodality_stats` exposes `bim_dip_p_embedding`, the three per-period dip
   p-values, `bim_gmm_separation` and `bim_gmm_modes`.
3. Rewrite §5.3 to the accepted text, every number a `{{< meta >}}` reference.
   Retitle it: "polarization" overclaims what the section can now support.
4. Fix the §5.4 closing clause — "the polarisation identified in §5.3" becomes
   the axis, since PC2's own delta_bic=118 carries the same caveat.
5. Repair `tests/test_multilayer_detection_pca.py`. It pins the old heading, so
   the retitle alone would break four tests with "heading not found". Its
   `test_mentions_dbic` also asserts a positive phrasing, which the project's
   own CI polarity rule forbids: positive pins break on every legitimate
   rewrite. Convert to a placeholder check plus a negative guard against
   re-asserting bimodality.

## Test

Red step — a negative guard, per the polarity rule:

    def test_53_does_not_claim_bimodality():
        """Ticket 0345. Dip p=1.0; §5.3 must not assert two modes."""
        section = extract_section(read(COMPANION), SECTION_53)
        assert not re.search(r"bimodal|two camps|two clusters", section, re.I), (
            "§5.3 asserts a bimodal structure the dip test does not support"
        )

Fails today on "reveals a bimodal structure".

